WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.737
Spark SQL does not execute your text directly in one simple step.

00:00:04.737 --> 00:00:13.277
It parses the SQL, resolves tables and columns, optimizes the logical plan, and creates a physical plan.

00:00:13.277 --> 00:00:18.972
The physical plan includes operators such as scans, filters, joins, aggregates, and exchanges.

00:00:18.972 --> 00:00:21.137
Exchanges often indicate data movement.

00:00:21.137 --> 00:00:26.022
When reading the example, do not read punctuation one character at a time.

00:00:26.022 --> 00:00:34.057
Start with the purpose of the query, then identify the input rows, the operation being applied,

00:00:34.057 --> 00:00:36.057
and the shape of the expected output.

00:00:36.057 --> 00:00:43.222
In a real setting such as a lakehouse processing millions of web events across many files,

00:00:43.222 --> 00:00:50.812
this habit makes code reviews much easier because you can explain what the query is supposed to guarantee

00:00:50.812 --> 00:00:53.087
before discussing syntax.

00:00:53.087 --> 00:00:59.200
After running it, inspect a few records manually and check that the result matches that explanation.
